      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 591552706,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "So under what circumstances will a received packet have more than one user?\n\nIn those circumstances, there's more than one data rate calculated; which one would be used for the duration calculation, or would the duration calculation need to use all of them?",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-06-02T23:40:25.421Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-06-02T23:53:48.411Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 102588678,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3228,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 591555975,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "I don't understand why radiotap allows multiple users, since when multiple users transmit at the same time they all transmit different packets in OFDMA. I've never seen more than one user.\n\nSimon",
          "author": {
            "id": 8966103,
            "username": "simon.barber",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Simon Barber",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/164e91fa9b6f381bf343eaa730c9c1b399c33bd3dadd8b5ec550dd8b2b7d2461?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/simon.barber"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-06-02T23:53:48.328Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-06-02T23:53:48.328Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 102588678,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3228,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "31554ab6e7f9ba1e5e2f9c330d9fc6aa11d1d214",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 591690568,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "So from what I've read about MU-MIMO, it sounds like a rough equivalent to switched Ethernet, in that it sounds as if an AP can transmit multiple signals at the same time, with the signals being beamformed so that they're directional (is that what a \"spatial stream\" is?) and don't interfere with one another (akin to a switch being able to transmit simultaneously on multiple ports) and a client (if capable) could transmit beam formed signals so that they're directional and don't interfere with other clients' beamformed signals, so that an AP can receive multiple signals at the same time (akin to a switch being able to receive simultaneously on multiple ports).\n\nPresumably a single packet wouldn't be broken into pieces and transmitted to multiple users (it might be transmitted in pieces to a *single* user).\n\nIf so, a STA would probably only be transmitting or receiving one signal at a time, so those packets would presumably always be marked as \"user 0\".\n\nFor an AP, it might be transmitting or receiving multiple spatial streams to or from multiple users (STAs), but any *given* packet would only be using one spatial stream, so each packet would only belong to one user, and all that might be useful would be to indicate which particular user it was from or to, so you might see multiple packets that overlapped in time (if you had both the start and end times, or had the start time and calculated the end time by adding the duration to the start time), and you could see which one was which user.\n\nEven if you have some passive sniffing device right on top of the AP, and *it* somehow managed to receive beamformed packets sent by and received by the AP (if that's possible), each packet would presumably belong to one user.\n\nAm I missing something here?  From some discussion between you and Johannes Berg in the \"HE (11ax) extensions\" radiotap mailing list thread back in February 2017, it sounds as if you're not going to see a single packet for multiple users:\n\n```\nOn Mon, 2017-02-13 at 11:42 -0800, Simon Barber wrote:\nIt’s not correctly implemented on captures from Macs - it increments\nfor every MPDU. TSF is reliable on all platforms that I’ve seen,\nalthough has different reference points for different platforms.\n\nOne things that is very odd is the different parameters for MU - 4\nsets, but I’ve only ever seen 1 MPDU reported. How is this supposed\nto be used?\n\nI don't think it *can* be used by a real sniffer, unless it is somehow\nable to decode for multiple users at once?\n\njohannes\n```\n\n(I'd point to the mailing list archives, but gmane.org appears to be down - the Wikipedia article about Gmane indicates that it moved to an NNTP-only news.gmane.io site.)",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-06-03T06:33:46.337Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-06-03T21:04:04.657Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 102588678,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3228,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 592492392,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Absolutely correct - I think this was a design mistake in radiotap. 11n includes Mu_MIMO, 11ax also allows OFDMA, but the exact same thing applies - each packet would be for a single user. I don't know of any situation where a single packet would be sent to multiple users at the same time. Hence my code assumes that only one user will be populated in this field.",
          "author": {
            "id": 8966103,
            "username": "simon.barber",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Simon Barber",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/164e91fa9b6f381bf343eaa730c9c1b399c33bd3dadd8b5ec550dd8b2b7d2461?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/simon.barber"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-06-03T21:04:04.568Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-06-03T21:04:04.568Z",
